\begin{abstract}
We report a diagnostic phenomenon in which a coarse-grained entity observable shows no non-monotonic extremum, while a fine-grained relational observable exhibits a sharp valley at the same parameter location. In the PXP model ($L=18$), the standard deviation of the half-chain entanglement spectrum jumps by $+7\%$ at chemical potential $\mu\approx1.5$, while the mean absolute spin correlation decreases monotonically. In a quantum many-body system generated from a Sierpi\'{n}ski fractal graph ($N=15$) by a relation-graph compiler, the standard deviation of edge correlation fluctuations drops by an absolute amount of $0.125$ ($60\%$ of its initial value) to a minimum of $0.0843$ at contradiction strength $s=0.50$, while the mean magnetization shows irregular fluctuations but no corresponding valley. This fractal valley depth is approximately $1.8$ times that of a ring graph and $2.6$ times that of a random graph of comparable size. Rastislav Draho\v{s} independently replicated the valley and confirmed it as a localized defect mode. Marat Sultanov's TAT-7 blind test confirmed the valley as a smooth modulation, not a sharp transition. We designate this valley the Li-Draho\v{s}-Sultanov Valley. Strong evidence is currently confined to the fractal graph; other topologies produce weaker signals to be analyzed separately. All data, codes, and protocols are publicly available.
\end{abstract}

