\section{The phenomenon}

We observe that in certain quantum many-body systems, a fine-grained relational observable exhibits a non-monotonic valley as a function of a local defect strength, while a coarse-grained entity observable shows no comparable non-monotonic feature. We call this Silent Discordance.

\subsection{Operational definition}

A default diagnosis is a coarse-grained entity observable: it averages over the entire system and is expected to capture the system's bulk behavior. An enhanced diagnosis is a fine-grained relational observable: it measures how strongly different parts of the system fluctuate in relation to each other, and is sensitive to the internal structure of correlations.

Silent Discordance is the simultaneous occurrence of three conditions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Enhanced non-monotonicity:} The enhanced diagnosis exhibits a non-monotonic extremum (valley, peak, or jump) whose amplitude significantly exceeds the natural fluctuations of adjacent intervals.
\item \textbf{Default blindness:} The default diagnosis shows no extremum at the same parameter location; it remains smooth or exhibits only irregular fluctuations unrelated to the enhanced extremum.
\item \textbf{Structural contrast:} The two diagnostics differ qualitatively, not merely quantitatively. The default diagnosis is not simply a rescaled version of the enhanced diagnosis; it lacks the non-monotonic feature altogether.
\end{enumerate}

No fixed amplitude threshold is implied. The defining signature is the structural contrast.

\subsection{Relation to existing diagnostics}

Silent Discordance is distinct from a quantum phase transition, in which the energy gap closes and a local order parameter exhibits a sharp change. It is distinct from an entanglement entropy singularity, which is typically a smooth function of the control parameter near a critical point. It is distinct from a scar-state crossing, in which a subset of eigenstates exhibits anomalous behavior while the rest of the spectrum remains thermal. Silent Discordance is a diagnostic phenomenon: it concerns the relationship between two ways of looking at the same system.

\subsection{Theoretical motivation}

The hypothesis that relational structure---not entity attributes---governs physical response has deep roots. The area law of entanglement entropy~\cite{Eisert2010} states that the information content of a region is determined by the area of its boundary, not its volume. Topological order is characterized by ground-state degeneracy that depends only on the global topology of the system~\cite{Wen2017}. Kitaev's description of Majorana zero modes as residing ``in the correlations between sites''~\cite{Kitaev2001} is an explicit statement of this view. Silent Discordance is a direct test: if the relational observable exhibits a non-monotonic feature while the entity observable does not, then relational structure carries independent dynamical content.

\section{Method}

\subsection{Hamiltonian and graph generation}

The Hamiltonian for the fractal graph and all control graphs is the isotropic Heisenberg model on a graph $G=(V,E)$:
\begin{equation}
H = \sum_{(i,j)\in E} J_{ij}\left(\sigma_i^x\sigma_j^x + \sigma_i^y\sigma_j^y + \sigma_i^z\sigma_j^z\right),
\end{equation}
where $J_{ij}=1$ for all edges except the contradiction edge, on which $J_{ij}=s$ is scanned from $0$ to $3.0$. The graph is generated by the ``Golden Staff'' relation-graph compiler from a user-specified topology. For the PXP model, a separate Hamiltonian is used, as described in Section~III.

\subsection{Diagnostics for the fractal graph and control graphs}

For the Heisenberg graph systems, the default diagnosis is the mean absolute magnetization:
\begin{equation}
D(s) = \left|\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N \langle \sigma_i^z \rangle_s\right|,
\end{equation}
where $\langle \cdot \rangle_s$ denotes the ground-state expectation value at contradiction strength $s$.

The enhanced diagnosis is the standard deviation of nearest-neighbor correlation fluctuations:
\begin{equation}
E(s) = \sqrt{\frac{1}{|E|}\sum_{(i,j)\in E}\left(\langle \sigma_i^z \sigma_j^z \rangle_s - \overline{\langle \sigma^z \sigma^z \rangle_s}\right)^2},
\end{equation}
where $\overline{\langle \sigma^z \sigma^z \rangle_s}$ is the mean correlation over all edges at strength $s$.

\subsection{Diagnostics for the PXP model}

For the PXP model, the default diagnosis is the mean absolute spin correlation (mean $|C|$), defined as the average of the absolute values of all spin-spin correlation matrix elements. The enhanced diagnosis is the standard deviation of the half-chain entanglement spectrum---the eigenvalues of the reduced density matrix of the left half of the chain.

\subsection{Choice of the Sierpi\'{n}ski gasket}

The Sierpi\'{n}ski gasket is chosen for two reasons. First, its finite ramification allows a clean separation between the defect neighborhood and the bulk: the three tip spins remain explicit under real-space coarse-graining, while the interior can be truncated without destroying the local defect mode. Second, the gasket is rich in triangular cycles, and control experiments show that valley depth increases with cycle density.

\subsection{Normative constraints}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Axiom I (Relation Ontology):} Entity composition is fixed; only the relational configuration is varied.
\item \textbf{Axiom II (Contradiction Dynamics):} The contradiction edge is the only non-uniformity.
\item \textbf{Axiom III (Practical Intervention):} Default and enhanced diagnostics are always displayed side by side.
\item \textbf{Axiom IV (Resonance Tuning):} No ``discovery'' is presumed as an optimization target.
\item \textbf{Axiom V (Meta-Reflection):} All boundaries and limitations are explicitly declared.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Preventing the tool-rationality paradox}

We adopt five preventive measures: (i) predefined success criteria fixed before execution; (ii) uniform parameters across runs; (iii) dual auditing for key data points; (iv) complete disclosure of data and code; (v) honest recording of negative results.

\subsection{Reproducibility audit}

All valley depths were subjected to a reproducibility audit with five independent Lanczos seeds. Valley positions are exactly reproducible; this establishes the position as an intrinsic geometric property of the relational field. Valley depths exhibit statistical scatter of $6\%$--$20\%$ across runs, reflecting sensitivity to the precise eigenvector selected within a degenerate or near-degenerate ground-state manifold. Depths are reported as mean $\pm$ standard deviation. No error bars are shown in figures because the scatter is a systematic spread from ground-state degeneracy, not statistical sampling uncertainty; the full five-run data are in the Supplementary Material.

\subsection{Independent corrections}

Rastislav Draho\v{s} contributed two critical corrections. First, an apparent correlation between spin-gap prefactor $A$ and a topological index $C$ was shown to be a mathematical tautology: $C$ is a deterministic function of chain length $L$ ($C \approx 0.9745 - 0.624/L$, $R^2 = 0.99999$). Second, a low-$\chi$ DMRG run on a periodic chain with a defect produced a spurious gap $A=0.47$; $\chi\to\infty$ extrapolation corrected it to $A=3.19\pm0.03$~\cite{Schollwock2011}.

\section{First observation in the PXP model}

\subsection{Setup}

The PXP model describes a one-dimensional Rydberg atom chain with the constraint that adjacent atoms cannot be simultaneously excited. We use $L=18$ (6765-dimensional constrained Hilbert space). A chemical potential defect $\mu$ is implanted at the central site and scanned from $0$ to $2.0$. The default diagnosis is the mean absolute spin correlation; the enhanced diagnosis is the standard deviation of the half-chain entanglement spectrum.

\subsection{Results}

The data are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:pxp} and Table~\ref{tab:pxp}. The default diagnosis decreases monotonically and smoothly from $0.0656$ to $0.0566$. The enhanced diagnosis decreases to a global minimum of $0.329742$ at $\mu=0.57$, then rises slowly, and at $\mu=1.43\to1.57$ jumps by $+7.0\%$ to $0.360240$---a single-step change more than three times the cumulative change of the preceding seven steps.

