We designate this valley the \textbf{Li-Draho\v{s}-Sultanov (LDS) Valley}, in recognition of the three independent diagnostic frameworks that confirmed it.

\subsection{Draho\v{s}'s independent replication and control experiments}

Rastislav Draho\v{s} replicated the fractal valley with independent code. He also identified and fixed a coding issue ($XX+ZZ$ instead of $XX+YY+ZZ$); the valley persisted after the fix. Control experiments on comparable graphs gave: ring graph depth $0.071$ at $s=1.00$; random graph depth $0.048$ at $s=2.00$; tree graph, no valley at comparable scan resolution\footnote{The tree-graph result was obtained at low scan resolution. A subsequent high-resolution study (to be reported separately) reveals a weak valley; this does not affect the present conclusions about the fractal graph.}. The LDS Valley depth ($0.125$) is $1.8$ times the ring and $2.6$ times the random graph.

\subsection{Local-probe analysis}

Draho\v{s} divided edges into local (near the defect) and bulk (distant). The local valley depth rose with increasing $\chi$, while the bulk contribution was diluted. The valley is a localized defect mode.

\subsection{Tensor-RG toolchain}

Draho\v{s} built a tensor-RG toolchain to test thermodynamic-limit survival~\cite{Drahos2026GitHub}. The energy RG is exact through L2. The impurity-explicit RG reproduces the L2 valley depth exactly. The L3 depth does not converge on available hardware. The full toolchain is publicly available.

\subsection{Contradiction-edge position}

Changing the contradiction edge from $(0,2)$ to $(0,1)$ completely eliminated the valley. The injection point is decisive.

\subsection{TAT blind test}

Sultanov's TAT ensemble analysis tested 13 metrics. TAT gave only boundary anchors and was silent at $s=0.50$, confirming the valley as a smooth modulation.

\section{Cycle enhancement: an empirical condition}

The control experiments support an empirical condition we call \emph{cycle enhancement}. Cycles are not strictly necessary, but they enhance stability, depth, and robustness. The fractal graph has the deepest valley ($0.125$); the ring and random graphs have shallower valleys ($0.071$ and $0.048$); the tree graph shows no valley at low resolution. We refrain from calling this a ``law'' because no analytical mechanism is yet established.

\section{Mechanism: a tentative picture}

The contradiction edge injects tension into the relational field. When injection is asymmetric---the two subgraphs separated by the edge are not equivalent---the field undergoes a sudden, localized reorganization of its correlation structure, appearing as a valley in the enhanced diagnosis. When injection is symmetric, tension is absorbed uniformly and no valley appears. This picture is consistent with the decisive effect of edge position, the enhancement by cycles, and the blindness of the default diagnosis. We present it only as a guide for future work, not as an established mechanism.

\section{Limitations}

\begin{enumerate}
\item All cross-graph scans except the fractal graph were at $N=6$.
\item All experiments were on a single personal computer.
\item All diagnostics were on the ground state.
\item Valley positions were located on 13-point grids and may shift with higher resolution.
\item Valley depths carry a systematic uncertainty of $6\%$--$20\%$.
\item The thermodynamic-limit behavior remains unresolved; a tensor-RG toolchain is available~\cite{Drahos2026GitHub}.
\item Strong evidence is currently confined to the fractal graph.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

We discovered Silent Discordance in the PXP model and provided strong evidence in a self-built fractal-graph quantum system. The LDS Valley has an absolute depth of $0.125$ ($60\%$ of curve amplitude), has been independently replicated, and is enhanced by cycles. The discovery was made under strict methodological constraints. The thermodynamic-limit question remains open and is explicitly scoped.

